<!-- MODULE 5: EXECUTIVE SUMMARY -->
<section class="cs-exec">
  <div class="cs-exec-inner">
    <p class="cs-exec-body">Luxury bedding occupies a specific position in the search landscape. The purchase value is high enough, typically several hundred dollars or more, that buyers spend time in consideration before committing. But the category intent is measurable: people do search explicitly for products like sateen duvet covers or linen sheet sets, and those searches represent real, convertible demand.</p>
    <p class="cs-exec-body">An account that serves that demand well can reach a stable level of performance without active intervention to maintain it. The challenge is that stability and potential are different things. An account can be genuinely healthy and still be operating below what a closer structural review would make possible.</p>
    <p class="cs-exec-body">Revenue was coming in, and nothing was obviously broken. The problem was quieter than that: performance had stopped improving.</p>
    <p class="cs-exec-body">Our review identified four structural constraints across customer data, product feeds, Search targeting, and campaign roles. None was dramatic in isolation. But in a high-AOV category such as luxury bedding, structural inefficiencies carry a meaningful cost when they persist across an entire year.</p>
    <p class="cs-exec-body">Over the documented 2023 period, the account attributed CA$1.55M in revenue from CA$151K in managed spend, a 10.26&times; blended ROAS. Performance Max recorded 9.38&times; across the year, while Search recorded 19.76&times;.</p>
  </div>
</section>


<!-- MODULE 8: WHAT WE FOUND -->
<section class="cs-findings">
  <div class="cs-findings-inner">
    <h2 class="cs-h2">What we found</h2>

    <div class="cs-findings-list" role="list">
      <div class="cs-finding" role="listitem">
        <span class="cs-finding-num">01</span>
        <div>
          <p class="cs-finding-title">First-party customer signals were unused</p>
          <p class="cs-finding-body">The client had thousands of verified purchasers in its CRM, but that data had never been uploaded to Google as a Customer Match audience signal. Without it, Performance Max had less direct information about the customers most valuable to the business. The campaign could still learn from conversion activity and other available signals, but the account wasn't providing Google with one of its strongest first-party reference points.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="cs-finding" role="listitem">
        <span class="cs-finding-num">02</span>
        <div>
          <p class="cs-finding-title">Product titles reflected internal language, not search demand</p>
          <p class="cs-finding-body">The feed had been built when the Shopify store launched and never updated. Titles used internal collection names and fabric codes rather than the language customers use when searching for luxury bedding. That limited product visibility across important high-intent queries.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="cs-finding" role="listitem">
        <span class="cs-finding-num">03</span>
        <div>
          <p class="cs-finding-title">Broad match was diluting Search intent</p>
          <p class="cs-finding-body">Search campaigns were reaching a wide mix of exploratory and comparison-oriented queries alongside buyers with clear intent, at the same bid level. We tightened targeting toward phrase and exact match where account evidence supported stronger purchase intent, concentrating Search budget around terms more closely associated with purchase behavior.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="cs-finding" role="listitem">
        <span class="cs-finding-num">04</span>
        <div>
          <p class="cs-finding-title">Performance Max and Search lacked clearly governed roles</p>
          <p class="cs-finding-body">The account did not have a clear framework for what Search should deliberately capture versus where Performance Max should provide broader product coverage. That made budget allocation, query analysis, and channel-level interpretation less straightforward.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="cs-mid-hook">
      <p class="cs-mid-hook-text">If your account is producing revenue but hasn't improved in several quarters, the constraint may not be traffic. It may be something quieter in the structure: customer signals Google isn't using, product data that doesn't match buyer language, or campaign roles that have grown less distinct over time.</p>
      <a href="../growth-diagnostic-landing.html#book" class="cs-mid-hook-link">That's what the Growth Diagnostic is designed to surface &rarr;</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

