    <h2 class="cs-h2">What we did</h2>

    <div class="cs-intervention-list">
      <div class="cs-intervention">
        <div class="cs-intervention-num" aria-hidden="true">01</div>
        <div>
          <div class="cs-intervention-title">Added Verified Purchaser Data as a Customer Match Audience Signal</div>
          <p class="cs-intervention-desc">We exported the client's verified purchaser list and applied it to Performance Max as a Customer Match audience signal. PMax now had a first-party reference point for the customers most valuable to the business, rather than learning solely from conversion activity and other platform-observed signals.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="cs-intervention">
        <div class="cs-intervention-num" aria-hidden="true">02</div>
        <div>
          <div class="cs-intervention-title">Rebuilt Product Feed Titles Around Buyer Search Language</div>
          <p class="cs-intervention-desc">Every title was rewritten to lead with what shoppers search for: "Luxury Cotton Duvet Cover Set [size] [material]" instead of internal collection names or fabric codes. Product visibility improved on the high-intent terms where the brand's catalog was most relevant.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="cs-intervention">
        <div class="cs-intervention-num" aria-hidden="true">03</div>
        <div>
          <div class="cs-intervention-title">Concentrated Search Budget Around Higher-Intent Queries</div>
          <p class="cs-intervention-desc">We moved Search campaigns away from broad match, tightening toward phrase and exact where account evidence supported it. Search budget became more concentrated around queries more closely associated with purchase behavior.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="cs-intervention">
        <div class="cs-intervention-num" aria-hidden="true">04</div>
        <div>
          <div class="cs-intervention-title">Clarified Strategic Roles Between Performance Max and Search</div>
          <p class="cs-intervention-desc">We built negative keyword lists to separate what PMax handles from what Search handles, and excluded wholesale, trade, and gift registry queries at the account level. Spend became easier to govern against each campaign's intended role.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="cs-callout">
      <p class="cs-callout-text">There was no single dramatic intervention in this account. The four adjustments were structural: clearer customer signals, product-feed language aligned with buyer search behavior, tighter Search targeting, and more clearly governed campaign roles. They were sustained consistently over twelve months. That is what shows up in the documented full-year performance.</p>
    </div>

  </div>
</section>

